Production analytic NAC regression: LiH / HF, R = 1.5 Angstrom, 10 Angstrom box.

This Autotest case executes the complete nacv.cpp path for S0-S1 and S1-S4,
including orbital response, force polarization, moving-AO and ETF terms.
nacv_overlap_fd.ref contains independent overlap finite differences, not values
copied from the analytic program. Both atoms and all Cartesian components are
covered. The included material files fix the complete 12-orbital reference.

Reference generation used centered displacements of 0.00025 and 0.000125 bohr.
Raw NAC = [<Phi_A(R)|Phi_B(R+h)> - <Phi_A(R)|Phi_B(R-h)>] / (2h).
The moving-AO term was differentiated from cross-geometry AO overlaps and
independent determinant tangents. ETF = raw_FD - AO_FD; no analytic AO term
was used to construct the expected values. overlap_fd_provenance.json records
both steps, the converged component values, model, original program identity
and original retained raw-frame directory. That external archive is provenance;
the fixed-file Autotest itself requires only the checked-in fixture.

Electronic eigenvectors have arbitrary signs. The generic file comparator uses
absolute component values for these nondegenerate roots; it never rescales or
fits a coupling. This detects component magnitude, spin-factor and polarization
regressions, but does not validate a common electronic gauge between runs.
The absolute tolerance is 1e-5 bohr^-1, stricter than the original reference
screen 1e-5 + 0.001*abs(FD). It is not a new FD calculation on every CI run.

Reproduce from tests/08_RI, with a LibRI-enabled CPU binary:
  OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 bash ../integrate/Autotest.sh -a /absolute/path/to/abacus -n 4 -r '^lr_nacv_hf_lih$'

records.compare specifies only the input file, record marker, selected columns,
reference file and numeric comparison settings. The validation-tools layer
contains no NAC definitions or other physical calculations.
